<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>1.10.5: Practice</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00230</md:content-id>
    <md:title>1.10.5: Practice</md:title>
    <md:uuid>3faa22a3-5670-4ccd-9da5-253e80672440</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><para>Complete the following questions to practice the skills you have learned in this lesson.</para>
<para>For questions 1 - 4, use the problem and graph below.</para>
<blockquote>Andre bought a new bag of cat food. The next day, he opened it to feed his cat. The graph shows how many ounces were left in the bag on the days after it was bought.

  <para><figure><media alt="Line on coordinate plane, origin O. Horizontal axis from 0 to 44 by 4’s, days since bag was bought. Vertical axis 0 to 64 by 8’s, ounces of cat food. Line passes through 0 comma 56 and 24 comma 16."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/8a41839540c92dd378bcb27baf29707c44cd53ac"/></media></figure></para>
</blockquote>
<div class="os-raise-ib-pset" data-button-text="Check" data-content-id="a9396f18-b066-4e89-9032-2e033c144f5e" data-fire-learning-opportunity-event="eventnameY" data-fire-success-event="eventnameX" data-retry-limit="2" data-schema-version="1.0">
  <!--Enter Questions Below this line-->
  <!--Q1-->
  <exercise data-content-id="5e9c977a-2ed2-4fbc-b91c-ef551070a162" data-problem-comparator="integer" data-problem-type="input" data-solution="36">
    <problem>
      <para>1. How many ounces of food were in the bag 12 days after Andre bought it?</para>
      <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
    </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! The answer is 36. When \(x\) is 12, the \(y\) value is 36.</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activity 1.10.2. </para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--Q2-->
  <exercise data-content-id="ce1fc39f-5784-4486-ace5-f73a9adf1b27" data-problem-comparator="integer" data-problem-type="input" data-solution="24">
    <problem>
      <para>2. How many days did it take for the bag to contain 16 ounces of food?</para>
      <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
    </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! The answer is 24. When \(y\) is 16, the\(x\) value is 24.</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activity 1.10.2.</para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--Q3-->
  <exercise data-content-id="79e55fd9-7a74-411f-93e3-bef6ec6a184e" data-problem-comparator="integer" data-problem-type="input" data-solution="56">
    <problem>
      <para>3. How much did the bag weigh before it was opened?</para>
      <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
    </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! The answer is 56. When \(x\) is 0, the \(y\) value is 56.</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activity 1.10.2. </para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--Q4-->
  <exercise data-content-id="59199d47-2608-4747-a948-aae74d7989d5" data-problem-comparator="integer" data-problem-type="input" data-solution="33">
    <problem>
      <para>4. Approximately many days did it take for the bag to be empty?</para>
      <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
    </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! The answer is 33. When \(y\) is 0, the\(x\) value is 33.</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activity 1.10.2.</para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--Do not edit below line.-->
  <solution>
    <!-- INSERT ANY VALID HTML HERE -->
  </solution>
  <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
    <!-- INSERT ANY VALID HTML HERE -->
  </div>
</div>

<para>For 5 - 7, use the problem and graph below.</para>
<blockquote>
  A group of hikers is progressing steadily along an uphill trail. The graph shows their elevation (or height above sea level), in feet, at each distance from the start of the trail, in miles.

  <figure><media alt="Graph of a line. Elevation, feet. Distance along trail, miles."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/b8eb3dec29ba5c5c9029dfa3cbe813a4df970361"/></media></figure></blockquote>

<div class="os-raise-ib-pset" data-button-text="Check" data-content-id="b64fa248-d06b-42ec-9c5f-5fb02127d54d" data-fire-learning-opportunity-event="eventnameY" data-fire-success-event="eventnameX" data-retry-limit="2" data-schema-version="1.0">
  <!--Enter Questions Below this line-->
  <!--q5-->
  <exercise data-content-id="f0eeaec7-569a-4f0f-9e9c-47917d747bb2" data-problem-comparator="integer" data-problem-type="input" data-solution="250">
    <problem>
      <para>5. What is the slope of the graph?</para>
      <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
    </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! The answer is 250. Use points \((0,500)\) and \((2,1000)\)</para>
      <para> \(m=\frac{1000-500}{2-1}=\frac{500}{2}=250\)</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activity 1.10.2.</para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--Q6-->
  <exercise data-content-id="5e6b1a80-5a13-4155-a621-7ce662c1d6ab" data-problem-type="dropdown" data-retry-limit="3" data-solution="The change in elevation as the hikers hike more miles." data-solution-options="[&quot;The change in elevation as the hikers hike more miles.&quot;, &quot;The change in miles as the elevation increases.&quot;,&quot;Only the distance along the trail (miles).&quot;,&quot;Only the elevation (feet).&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>6. What does the slope tell us about this situation?</para>
    </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! The answer is the change in elevation as the hikers hike more miles.</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activity 1.10.2. </para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  
  <!--Q7-->
  <exercise data-content-id="1274bf04-0581-4f5e-8d9b-2bc79be0c9d5" data-problem-comparator="math" data-problem-type="input" data-solution="y=250x+500">
    <problem>
      <para>7. Enter the equation that represents the relationship between the hikers' distance from the start of the trail, \(x\), and their elevation, \(y\), using the equation editor.</para>
    </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! The equation is \(y=250x+500\). This equation indicates that for every unit increase in \(x\), the elevation \(y\) increases by 250 units, starting from an initial elevation of 500.</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Remember to consider how the distance \(x\) affects the elevation \(y\).</para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  
  <!--Q8-->
  <exercise data-content-id="8c6856b2-7689-47d8-8f42-5e534abb7c33" data-problem-type="dropdown" data-retry-limit="3" data-solution="0 sheets of stickers and 6 packs of cardstock were bought." data-solution-options="[&quot;0 sheets of stickers and 6 packs of cardstock were bought.&quot;, &quot;6 sheets of stickers and 0 packs of cardstock were bought.&quot;,&quot;7 sheets of stickers and 3 packs of cardstock were bought.&quot;,&quot;14 sheets of stickers and 6 packs of cardstock were bought.&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>8. A kindergarten teacher bought $21 worth of stickers and cardstock for his class. The stickers cost $1.50 a sheet and the cardstock cost $3.50 per pack. The equation \(1.5s + 3.5c =21\) represents the relationship between sheets of stickers, \(s\), packs of cardstocks, \(c\), and the dollar amount a kindergarten teacher spent on these supplies.
        What does the vertical intercept, (0,6), mean in this situation?
      </para>
      <figure><media alt="Line on grid, origin O. Horizontal axis, 0 to 18 by 2’s, sheets of stickers. Vertical axis, 0 to 10 by 2’s, packs of cardstock. Line passes through points 0 comma 6, 7 comma 3, and 14 comma 0."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/9ab02a96ee7d70b167808f620e20c29384caa813"/></media></figure></problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! The answer is 0 sheets of stickers and 6 packs of cardstock were bought.</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activity 1.10.2.</para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  
